1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Nf3 (3. f4 exf4 4. Nf3 g5 5. d4 g4 6. Bc4 gxf3 7. O-O d5
$1 8. exd5 Bg4 $1 9. Re1+ Nge7 $1 10. gxf3 Bh3 $1 11. Bxf4 Rg8+ 12. Bg3 Na5 13.
Bb5+ c6 14. Ne4 $1 Bg7 $1 15. Kh1 Qxd5 16. c4 Qd7 17. d5 $1 Kf8 18. d6 Nf5 $1
19. Nc5 $1 Nxg3+ 20. hxg3 Qf5 21. d7 Bd4 $3 22. Nxb7 $1 Qh5 $1 23. Re8+ Rxe8
24. dxe8=Q+ Kxe8 25. Qe2+ Kf8 26. Nxa5 Bg4+ {0-1, Milner-Barry-Alexander,
Cambridge 1930s}) 3... Nf6 4. d4 (4. Bb5 Nd4 5. Ba4 Bc5 6. Nxe5 O-O 7. Nd3 Bb6
8. e5 Ne8 9. Nd5 d6 10. c3 Qh4 $1 11. Ne3 Qe4 12. Nb4 dxe5 $1 13. cxd4 exd4 14.
Qc2 Qe5 15. f4 $2 Qxf4 $1 16. Nc4 Qh4+ 17. Kf1 d3 $1 18. Nxd3 Bf5 $1 19. Nxb6
axb6 20. Bb3 Rd8 {0-1, Tylor-Milner Barry, Hastings 38/39.}) 4... Bb4 {[The
so-called Yugoslav Defence to the Scotch Four Knights, a welcome departure
from the routine 4...exd4.]} 5. d5 {[This move must, on general strategic
grounds, be regarded as rather nebulous, since it releases the tension in the
centre. However, as 5 d5 is not played here with the idea of a closed game to
follow, this objection may be dropped.]} (5. dxe5 Nxe4 {is quite harmless.}) ({
On} 5. Nxe5 {, recommended by the theoreticians, Black can either play for
safe equality with} Qe7 ({or if in a more enterprising mood, seek
complications with the uncharted} 5... O-O) ({he may even (Phillips) risk} 5...
Nxe4 $6 6. Qg4 Nxc3 {and now} 7. Qxg7 (7. a3 $2 Qf6 $1 {e.g.} (7... Nxe5 $2 8.
dxe5 Bf8 9. bxc3 d6 10. Qe4 {draw agreed(?), Barden-Phillips, 2nd match game
1954. As Barden himself points out, the peaceful decision at the end of the
beginning might well have been premature, in view of} dxe5 11. Qxe5+ Be7 $1 {
with play}) 8. axb4 Nxe5 9. dxe5 Qxe5+ 10. Be3 Nd5 {with an edge}) 7... Rf8 ({
Just to make the verdict on 5...Nxe5?! even more obscure, Black has} 7... Ne4+
8. c3 Qf6 9. Qxf6 Nxf6 10. cxb4 Nxd4 11. Bd3 d6 {which appears to give him a
tolerable game}) 8. a3 Ba5 (8... Nxd4 9. axb4 Nxc2+ 10. Kd2 Nxa1 11. Kxc3 $18 {
Euwe}) 9. Nxc6 (9. Bd2 Ne4) 9... dxc6 10. Qe5+ Qe7 11. Qxe7+ Kxe7 12. Bd2 Bf5 {
and it seems that White can obtain a minimal edge with} 13. Bxc3 ({Kmoch,
evidently not satisfied with the result of this unambitious line, has since
suggested} 13. Bd3 Bxd3 14. cxd3 {as an improvement for White, with the
following interesting eventualities:} Nb5 (14... Rad8 15. bxc3 (15. Bxc3 Bb6)
15... c5 16. c4 $1 Bxd2+ 17. Kxd2 Rxd4 (17... cxd4 18. Rhe1+ {with Re4 and
Rae1 to follow}) 18. Rhe1+ Kf6 19. Re3 Rfd8 20. Kc3 $16) ({(Phillips)} 14...
Nd5) 15. Bxa5 Nxd4 (15... Ke6 $5) 16. Bb4+ Kd7 17. Bxf8 Nc2+ 18. Kd2 Nxa1 19.
Bb4 a5 20. Bc3 Nb3+ 21. Kc2 Nc5 22. Re1 $14) 13... Bxc3+ 14. bxc3 Bxc2 15. Kd2
{Alexander-Phillips, Ilford 1953})) 5... Ne7 (5... Nxe4 $6 6. Qd3 (6. dxc6)
6... Nc5 7. Qc4 b5 $5 8. Qg4 $16 (8. Qxb5 $4 Ba6)) 6. Nxe5 (6. Bd2 d6 7. Bd3
O-O 8. a3 Ba5 9. b4 Bb6 10. Na4 Nexd5 11. exd5 e4 12. Nxb6 axb6 $11 {
Stumpers-Euwe, NED-ch 1952}) 6... d6 $6 (6... O-O $6 7. Qd4 $1 Bxc3+ 8. bxc3
Re8 $1 9. Bg5 Nf5 $1 10. exf5 d6 11. f4 dxe5 12. fxe5 Qxd5 13. O-O-O $1 Qxd4 (
13... Qxa2 $6 14. Bc4 {or 14 Bd3 White's central preponderance should tell in
his favour sometime} (14. exf6 $2 Bxf5 $1 15. Bd3 Re2 $3 $19)) 14. cxd4 Ne4 15.
Bh4 Nc3 $1 {with a position difficult to assess} 16. Rd3 Nxa2+ 17. Kb2 Nb4 18.
Rf3 a5 19. c3 Nd5 20. Bc4 c6 21. g4 b5 22. Bd3 Bd7 23. g5 c5 24. Rhf1 a4 25.
Be4 Bc6 26. g6 a3+ 27. Ka1 cxd4 28. gxf7+ Kxf7 29. e6+ Kg8 30. cxd4 Ra4 31. Rg1
Rxd4 32. f6 Rxe4 33. f7+ Kf8 34. fxe8=Q+ Kxe8 35. Bf2 b4 36. Bc5 Rxe6 37. Rf8+
Kd7 38. Rxg7+ Ne7 39. Bxb4 Bd5 40. Rff7 {1-0 Persitz-CHO'D Alexander, Swindon
1954}) (6... Nxe4 $2 7. Qd4) 7. Bb5+ $6 (7. Nf3 Nxe4 8. Qd4 Bxc3+ (8... Nxc3 $6
9. bxc3 Bc5 10. Qxg7 Rg8 11. Qxh7 (11. Qf6 Nxd5 12. Qxd8+ {Clarke-Phillip,
Oslo 1954}) 11... Bf5 12. Qh4 Qd7 13. Bg5 O-O-O $1 $13) 9. bxc3 Nf6 10. c4 O-O
11. Be2 (11. Bd3 Nf5) 11... Re8 {Tarrasch & Others-Paulsen, 1888}) 7... c6 8.
dxc6 O-O 9. Nd7 Nxe4 10. O-O Nxc3 11. bxc3 Bxc3 12. cxb7 Bxb7 13. Rb1 Re8 14.
Nf6+ Bxf6 15. Bxe8 Bd5 16. Ba4 Bxa2 17. Rb7 Qc8 18. Rd7 Nd5 19. Qf3 Nb6 20. Bc6
Nxd7 21. Bxa8 Nb6 22. Be4 d5 23. Qa3 Bc4 24. Bd3 Qc7 25. Re1 Be5 26. Bf4 f6 27.
Bf5 Kf7 28. Qh3 Ke7 29. Qh4 Kd6 30. Rxe5 {Sources: BCM, December 1955, p369 
(with notes); CHESS, 30 September 1955, p455} 1-0

